{
  "dom_id": "step-q96596",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $A$ is a symmetric matrix, then $A+{A}^{2}$ is always symmetric.\n- The product of *any* two $n\\times n$ matrices is always symmetric.\n- If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$  matrix, then ${A}^{2}$ is always symmetric.",
  "worked_solution": "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $A$ is symmetric, then ${A}^{2}$ is symmetric. And, since the sum to two symmetric matrices is symmetric, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+{A}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis symmetric.\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nHowever, their product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nHowever, ${A}^{2}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
